      "fact_text": {
        "text": "to declare an array of integers you write int[] followed by the variable name",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "arrays are reference types and must be created with the new operator",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "the size (length) of an array is specified in square brackets after the new operator",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "array indices start at 0; the first element is accessed with index 0",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "accessing an index outside the array bounds (e.g., index 10 in a length‑5 array) throws an exception and crashes the program",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "printing an array directly (e.g., System.out.println(array)) yields a string based on the object's memory address, not its contents",
        "refs": ["Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "the java.util.Arrays class provides a toString method that returns a readable representation of an array's items",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "the Arrays.toString method is overloaded for each primitive type and for reference types",
